<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    provenance={meta}
    route={['Spain', 'Catalonia', 'Barcelona']}
    label="Food · The System and the Table"
    title="Barcelona Food Guide:"
    titleEmphasis="How the City Eats, and What to Order"
    standfirst="Not a ranked list of restaurants. Barcelona collapses four or five separate food traditions into the word tapas, and the useful skill is recognising what kind of place you are standing in, what belongs on its counter, and what hour it wants you to arrive."
    stats={[
      { num: '39', label: 'Food Markets' },
      { num: '14:00', label: 'Lunch Starts' },
      { num: '21:30', label: 'Dinner Starts' },
      { num: '5', label: 'Documented Tables' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          The word that ruins Barcelona for visitors is <em>tapas</em>. It is not
          that tapas do not exist here — they do — but that the word has been
          made to carry four or five unrelated things at once, and a traveller
          holding one word for five traditions cannot tell them apart. Catalan
          home cooking is not tapas. A Basque pintxo bar in the Eixample is not
          Catalan. A plate of <em>conserves</em> eaten standing at a marble
          counter in Poble-sec belongs to a tradition of tinned seafood that has
          nothing to do with fried Andalusian bar food. And the thing sold on
          La Rambla under a photograph of paella belongs to none of them.
        </p>
        <p>
          So this is a guide to the system rather than a directory. It sets out
          what the traditions actually are, the clock the city keeps, the words
          you need to order in the right units, a focused vocabulary of dishes
          worth crossing a room for, an honest reading of the four markets that
          matter, and how the table changes as you move between
          neighbourhoods. The question it answers is not which ten places to
          book. It is: <em>what kind of place is this, what should I order in
          it, and what time does it want me?</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also unusually careful about what it claims to have eaten, for
          reasons set out below. Five establishments appear in the record of the
          stays behind this site. Some were sat down in; one is recorded as a
          recommendation and photographed from the pavement; not one has an
          exact order written down anywhere. Rather than invent five meals to
          make the prose richer, this page labels each claim for what it is.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

