  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter
      id="what-it-is"
      number="I."
      name="What Barcelona Food Actually Is"
      epithet="Five traditions sharing one city, and the reason a single word cannot hold them"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Start with the region rather than the city. <strong>Catalan
        cooking</strong> is one of the old regional kitchens of the western
        Mediterranean and it has a recognisable grammar: a base of slowly
        softened onion and tomato; the nut-and-bread thickeners that turn a
        broth into a sauce; the habit of putting fruit with meat and salt cod
        with vegetables; and a genuine reverence for grilled and roasted
        vegetables that most of Spain does not share. It is not a light cuisine
        and it is not a fiery one. It is a patient one, and the phrase you will
        see on menus — <em>cuina de mercat</em>, market cooking — is a claim
        about sourcing rather than style.
      </p>
      <p>
        That grammar has names, and knowing four of them makes a Catalan menu
        legible. A <strong>sofregit</strong> is the foundation — onion, and often
        tomato, cooked down slowly until it is jam rather than vegetable; almost
        every stew in the region starts here, and the difference between a good
        kitchen and a lazy one is how long it was given. A
        <strong>picada</strong> is the finish: nuts, garlic, fried bread, parsley
        and sometimes chocolate pounded together and stirred in at the end to
        thicken and perfume a sauce, which is why Catalan stews have body without
        flour or cream. <strong>Samfaina</strong> is the region's answer to
        ratatouille, and <strong>romesco</strong> the pounded sauce of roasted
        peppers, almonds and bread that arrives with grilled things and with
        calçots. Sofregit at the start, picada at the end: that is the shape of
        most of what you will eat.
      </p>
      <p>
        Onto that sits <strong>market culture</strong>, which in Barcelona is
        unusually institutional. The city runs its markets through a municipal
        body, the Institut Municipal de Mercats, set up in 1991; there are
        <strong>thirty-nine food markets</strong> and four non-food ones, and
        the network is dense enough that almost every neighbourhood has one
        within walking distance. That matters to a visitor for a reason that is
        not romantic: it means the good produce is distributed across the whole
        city, so there is no need to travel to a famous market to eat well.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then the formats. <strong>Tapes</strong> — small plates — are real here
        but less central than in Andalucía, and the Catalan habit leans towards
        slightly larger shared plates. <strong>Pintxos</strong> bars are
        everywhere and are a Basque import: very good, worth an evening, and not
        evidence of anything about Catalan food.
        <strong>Conserves</strong> — high-quality tinned seafood, mussels,
        cockles, anchovies, tuna belly — are treated here as a delicacy rather
        than a compromise, and the standing bars that serve them on bread are
        the format most specific to this city.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two more strands complete the picture. The <strong>sea</strong> gives
        Barcelona salt cod, anchovies, small fried fish, and the rice and noodle
        dishes discussed below — though the city's relationship with paella is
        complicated and is dealt with under mistakes.
        And <strong>immigration and the contemporary restaurant scene</strong>
        have made the last two decades the most interesting in the city's cooking
        since the war.
      </p>
      <p>
        That last strand deserves more than a clause, because it is where
        Barcelona now differs most from its own reputation. The Catalan avant-garde
        of the 1990s and 2000s trained a generation, and when those kitchens
        closed or contracted their cooks did not leave — they opened small rooms
        of twenty or thirty covers across Sant Antoni, Poble-sec, Gràcia and the
        Eixample, cooking recognisably Catalan food with technique borrowed from
        far grander places. Running alongside it is a second layer with nothing to
        do with fine dining: Pakistani and Bangladeshi kitchens through El Raval,
        Filipino and Latin American cooking in Sant Antoni and Poble-sec, and a
        bakery revival that has quietly made this one of the better cities in
        Europe for bread. Neither layer is what the guidebooks describe, and
        together they are the reason a week here is not repetitive.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Beat>Five traditions, one word, and a menu that will not explain the difference to you.</Beat>

    <Chapter
      id="the-clock"
      number="II."
      name="The Clock"
      epithet="The single largest cause of a disappointing meal here, and it has nothing to do with the cooking"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        More visitors eat badly in Barcelona because of the hour than because of
        the address. A kitchen serving at seven in the evening is, with few
        exceptions, a kitchen serving people who do not live here. The rhythm,
        loosely, runs like this.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Breakfast</strong> is small and early — coffee standing at a bar
        with a pastry, or bread with tomato. It is not an event.
        <strong>Second breakfast</strong>, mid-morning, is the one visitors never
        expect: a sandwich or a small plate around eleven, which is why bars are
        busy at an hour that seems to belong to nothing.
        <strong>Lunch</strong> is the real meal. Kitchens fill from about two and
        are busy until half past three; arriving at one o'clock gets you a room
        that has not started, and arriving at four gets you a kitchen closing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>afternoon</strong> is where the generalisations break down.
        Plenty of central places now trade straight through, and it is no longer
        safe to assume the whole city shuts between four and seven — but the
        neighbourhood dining rooms worth finding do close, and the market
        counters certainly do. <strong>Vermut</strong> occupies the slot before a
        meal: late morning at weekends, or from about seven in the evening.
        <strong>Dinner</strong> begins at half past nine and a nine o'clock
        booking is early. In August, when the heat pushes everything later, a
        terrace at eleven is ordinary.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two practical consequences. First, a day of sightseeing should be built
        so that lunch lands at two rather than being squeezed in at noon — this
        is the reason
        <a href="/one-day-in-barcelona/">the single-day route</a> puts its meal
        where it does. Second, do not publish your own schedule onto the city:
        if you need to eat at seven, eat at a market counter or a pintxos bar,
        both of which are genuinely open, rather than at a restaurant that will
        cook for you resentfully.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/placa-del-pi-before-the-tables.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/placa-del-pi-before-the-tables-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/placa-del-pi-before-the-tables-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/placa-del-pi-before-the-tables-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/placa-del-pi-before-the-tables.jpg 1365w`}
