    <Chapter
      id="formats"
      number="III."
      name="Ordering Formats"
      epithet="The words that decide how much food arrives, and how much it costs to sit down"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Getting the units right matters more than getting the dish right. A
        table that orders six <em>racions</em> thinking they are tapas will be
        defeated by the third.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <strong>tapa</strong> is a small plate for one. A
        <strong>platillo</strong> — the word you will meet most often in
        Barcelona — is a slightly larger plate designed to be shared between two
        or three; most of the city's good small-plate restaurants are built on
        these, and four or five between two people is a meal. A
        <strong>ració</strong> (<em>ración</em>) is a full sharing plate, and two
        of them plus bread will feed two people properly. Do not import the
        Andalusian three-size ladder here; Barcelona's working distinction is
        simply small plate, sharing plate, and main.
      </p>
      <p>
        A <strong>montadito</strong> is a construction on a slice of bread,
        built to order — the format at the standing conserves bars, where the
        bread is a plate rather than a sandwich. A <strong>pintxo</strong> is its
        Basque cousin, usually pinned with a skewer and often laid out along the
        counter for you to take; the sticks are counted at the end, so keep them.
        The etiquette differs: at a pintxos bar you serve yourself and are
        trusted, at a conserves bar you ask and wait.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>menú del dia</strong> is the best-value meal in Spain and it
        is a weekday lunchtime institution: a starter, a main, bread, a drink
        and usually a dessert or coffee at one fixed price. It exists to feed
        people who are working. You will not find it advertised in English, it
        will not be on a website, and the board outside is often chalk. If you
        want one good Barcelona habit from this page, it is to eat one of these
        on a Tuesday.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, the geography of the room. Standing at the
        <strong>bar counter</strong> is normal, sociable and sometimes cheaper.
        A <strong>table</strong> inside is the ordinary choice. A
        <strong>terrace</strong> table frequently carries a surcharge, which is
        legal and should be posted. And a <strong>market stall</strong> is not a
        restaurant: some counters cook and have stools, most sell you raw
        ingredients and will be puzzled to be asked for lunch.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Chapter
      id="what-to-order"
      number="IV."
      name="What to Order"
      epithet="A short vocabulary that is genuinely Catalan, and where each thing makes sense"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        This is deliberately not a dictionary. Twelve things, each with a note
        on where ordering it makes sense, which is the part most lists leave out.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pa amb tomàquet</strong> — bread rubbed with ripe tomato, oil and
        salt. The foundation of the Catalan table and the test of a kitchen's
        seriousness about small things. Order it everywhere; it arrives with
        almost everything.
        <strong>Escalivada</strong> — aubergine and pepper roasted until they
        collapse, skinned, dressed in oil, served cool. The best argument that
        Catalan cooking is a vegetable cuisine. Best in an ordinary dining room
        rather than anywhere modern.
        <strong>Esqueixada</strong> — salt cod torn raw into strips and cured in
        its dressing with tomato, onion and olives. A summer dish, and the one
        that most surprises visitors expecting cod to be cooked.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Botifarra amb mongetes</strong> — the Catalan sausage grilled and
        served with white beans. Plain, and a whole regional identity in one
        plate. A neighbourhood restaurant dish; be suspicious of it on a
        tasting menu.
        <strong>Cap i pota</strong> — slow-cooked veal head and trotter, gelatinous
        and deeply savoury, on menús del dia in old dining rooms and almost
        nowhere aimed at visitors. If you see it, someone in that kitchen is
        cooking for locals.
        <strong>Bombes</strong> — a fried potato ball with a spiced meat centre,
        Barceloneta's own invention, best eaten in that quarter standing up.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Conserves</strong> — tinned mussels, cockles, razor clams,
        anchovies, tuna belly, opened at the counter and eaten on bread. Do not
        read tinned as second-rate; the best of it costs more than fresh fish and
        is the most characteristic thing you can eat in this city.
        <strong>Suquet de peix</strong> — the fishermen's stew, potatoes and fish
        in a broth thickened the Catalan way. A lunch dish, and better near the
        water.
        <strong>Arròs and fideuà</strong> — Catalan rice dishes, and the same
        thing built with short noodles instead of rice. <em>Arròs negre</em>, made
        black with cuttlefish ink, is the one to order. Both belong at a proper
        lunch, cooked to order, never sitting under a lamp.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Crema catalana</strong> — the burnt-sugar custard, older and
        looser than a crème brûlée and flavoured with citrus and cinnamon.
        <strong>Mel i mató</strong> — fresh unsalted curd cheese with honey and
        nuts, which is the dessert to order when you have eaten too much
        already.
        And <strong>vermut</strong> — sweet red vermouth over ice with a splash
        of soda, an olive and something salty. Not a cocktail and not an
        aperitif in the fussy sense; a small ritual with an hour attached, and
        the easiest local habit to adopt.
      </p>
      <p>
        One seasonal entry, with the honesty it needs.
        <strong>Calçots</strong> are long sweet spring onions charred whole over
        vine cuttings, peeled by hand and dipped in romesco, and a
        <em>calçotada</em> is among the best meals in Catalonia. The season runs
        roughly <strong>November to April</strong> and peaks between January and
        March; the heartland is the countryside inland from Tarragona rather than
        the city. In Barcelona in August they are simply not available, and any
        menu offering them then is telling you something about itself.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="what-to-drink" level={3}>And what to drink with it</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Catalonia makes its own wine and Barcelona drinks it, which means the
        house pour is usually local and usually fine. Two regions do most of the
        work. <strong>Penedès</strong>, half an hour south-west of the city, is
        where the whites and the sparkling wine come from: this is
        <strong>cava</strong> country, and the most useful thing to understand
        about cava is that here it is an everyday wine rather than a celebration
        one. A glass with lunch is ordinary. <strong>Priorat</strong>, further
        inland, makes the serious reds — dense, mineral, expensive, grown on
        slate terraces — and a bottle of it is a decision rather than a default.
        Between them sit <strong>Empordà</strong> in the north and the
        everyday reds of <strong>Montsant</strong>, which is where most
        good-value house wine actually comes from. Penedès is also the one wine
        region close enough to visit between breakfast and dinner — forty-five
        minutes on a stopping train, and assessed as
        <a href="/best-day-trips-from-barcelona/">a day trip in its own
        right</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Beyond wine, three habits. <strong>Vermut</strong>, as above, is the
        pre-meal ritual and the easiest to adopt. <strong>Beer</strong> is
        ordered small — ask for a <em>canya</em> and you get a modest draught
        glass, which is the point, since it stays cold. And if you are handed a
        <strong>porró</strong> — a glass vessel with a long spout, drunk at arm's
        length without touching your lips — you are being invited into something
        rather than tested. Aim for your mouth, not your shirt.
      </p>
    </div>

    <PullQuote>
      Tinned is not a compromise here. The best conserves cost more than the
      fresh fish beside them, and eating them on bread standing up is the most
      Barcelona thing on this page.
    </PullQuote>

    <Chapter
      id="markets"
      number="V."
      name="The Markets"
      epithet="Four of the thirty-nine, judged on what each one is actually for"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The mistake with Barcelona's markets is treating them as attractions
        rather than as infrastructure with four different functions: a shopping
        hall, a place to eat something prepared, a spectacle, and a
        neighbourhood institution. Most are all four in different proportions.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also worth knowing how to use one, because the etiquette is not
        obvious. Stalls are specialists, not departments — the fishmonger does
        not sell you bread, and you will visit four counters to assemble a lunch.
        At busy stalls you take a numbered ticket or simply ask
        <em>qui és l'últim?</em> and wait your turn; pushing to the glass is the
        one thing that will earn you a hard look. Quantities are given in
        <em>grams</em>, and asking for a slice or two of something is entirely
        normal. And almost every market has at least one bar built into it, which
        opens when the traders do and is among the best-value breakfasts in the
        city — a counter, a coffee, and something on bread at seven in the
        morning, surrounded by people who have already been working for hours.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="boqueria" level={3} rule={false}>La Boqueria</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Both the most famous market in Spain and a genuinely working one, and
        the honest position is not that it has been ruined but that it has two
        distinct zones. The first aisles off La Rambla are the spectacle: fruit
        cups, cones of cured ham, juices, things designed to be photographed and
        eaten walking. Go past them. Behind sit the fish counters, the mushroom
        specialist, the offal, the salt cod, the egg stall, the pickles — a
        serious market that supplies restaurants. It trades
        <strong>Monday to Saturday, 08:00–20:30</strong> and is
        <strong>closed on Sundays and public holidays</strong>; before eleven is
        a different and better place than noon. Details on
        <a href="https://www.boqueria.barcelona/" rel="noopener">the market's own
        site</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Verdict:</em> shopping market with a tourist frontage. Worth twenty
        minutes for the back half, and it has a handful of counters that cook
        properly if you can get a stool.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/boqueria-jamon-cones.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/boqueria-jamon-cones-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/boqueria-jamon-cones-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/boqueria-jamon-cones-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/boqueria-jamon-cones-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/boqueria-jamon-cones.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A row of paper cones filled with slices of cured ham in a lit market display case, photographed at a shallow angle so the further cones dissolve into coloured bokeh, with vacuum-packed sliced ham and a printed price label on the counter in the foreground."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="The front row of La Boqueria, doing exactly what the front row is for. Nothing here is dishonest and none of it is why the market exists — the fish and the salt cod are forty metres further in."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="sant-antoni" level={3}>Mercat de Sant Antoni</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The one to go to if you want a market rather than a market visit. It sits
        in a vast iron and brick hall of 1882 at the corner of the Eixample, was
        rebuilt over most of a decade and reopened with the Roman and medieval
        remains found underneath left visible. It is three markets in one: the
        food hall, the surrounding <em>encants</em> of clothing and haberdashery,
        and a Sunday book and collectors' trade. Note the day —
        <strong>it closes on Tuesdays</strong>, and the stalls broadly run
        10:00–20:00 otherwise, which is worth confirming on
        <a href="https://www.mercatdesantantoni.com/en/" rel="noopener">the
        market's own site</a> before making a journey.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Verdict:</em> neighbourhood institution, and the best of these four
        for simply watching a city buy its dinner. The bars on the streets around
        it are the real reward.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="santa-caterina" level={3}>Mercat de Santa Caterina</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        <em>Assessed rather than visited.</em> Five minutes from the cathedral,
        and the most architecturally interesting of the four: Enric Miralles and
        Benedetta Tagliabue wrapped the old hall in an undulating roof of some
        325,000 ceramic pieces in 67 colours, meant to echo the produce beneath.
        Its opening was delayed by four years — planned for 2001, reopened in
        <strong>May 2005</strong> — because the excavation turned up the church
        and convent of Santa Caterina, whose Romanesque apse and burial ground
        are now preserved as a MUHBA space inside the market. Its hours are
        mixed, with early closing on some days and two late evenings, so check
        before relying on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Verdict:</em> a working neighbourhood market with a remarkable roof
        and an archaeological site in the basement. The obvious choice if you are
        already in the old city and want a market without the Boqueria's crowd.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="llibertat" level={3}>Mercat de la Llibertat</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        <em>Assessed rather than visited.</em> Gràcia's market, and the one that
        makes the case for the network rather than the landmark. The hall was
        inaugurated in <strong>1888</strong> to a design by Gràcia's own
        municipal architect, and its modernista ironwork was decorated by
        Francesc Berenguer i Mestres — Gaudí's long-time collaborator — which
        makes it, quietly, one of the Modernisme buildings in this city that
        nobody queues for. Stalls run roughly Monday to Saturday, 09:00–20:00.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Verdict:</em> shopping market, no spectacle, and the right answer if
        you are staying in Gràcia. Pair it with an evening in the squares
        described below.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/boqueria-charcuterie-stall.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/boqueria-charcuterie-stall-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/boqueria-charcuterie-stall-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/boqueria-charcuterie-stall-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/boqueria-charcuterie-stall-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/boqueria-charcuterie-stall.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A charcuterie stall in La Boqueria in Barcelona under pink and orange light: whole cured hams hanging in rows, bagged fuet, chorizo and llonganissa strung across the front with large orange handwritten price tags, a chilled counter of sliced meats and cheeses below, and a woman with her back to the camera standing at the glass."
      width={2048}
      height={1463}
      caption="The vocabulary is on the tags: fuet, llonganissa de pagès, chorizo. The prices are the ones that hung there on the day and are no guide to today's — but the range between an everyday fuet and a leg of acorn-fed ham is the lasting lesson."
    />

    <Chapter
      id="neighbourhoods"
      number="VI."
      name="Neighbourhoods at the Table"
      epithet="The same city eating five different ways, and which one suits the evening you want"
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Where you eat in Barcelona changes what eating means, and the differences
        are sharper than the distances suggest. This is not a repeat of
        <a href="/best-areas-to-stay-in-barcelona/">the accommodation
        assessment</a> — it is only about the table.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>old city</strong> — the Barri Gòtic, El Born, El Raval — has
        the widest quality range in the smallest area. El Born holds the densest
        concentration of small serious kitchens; the Gòtic has both
        century-old xarcuteries and the worst value in Barcelona within fifty
        metres of each other; El Raval is where the immigrant kitchens and the
        cheapest good eating are, and it does not flatter anyone. The rule in all
        three: turn off the wide street.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <strong>Eixample</strong> eats formally. This is where the
        contemporary restaurants and the business lunches are, the rooms are
        larger, the pavement tables sit under plane trees, and the food is more
        expensive and often better. It is also where a menú del dia is easiest to
        find at a decent standard, because it is feeding office workers.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Sant Antoni and Poble-sec</strong> are, for eating, the best
        ground in the city. Sant Antoni has the market and the young kitchens
        that followed it; Poble-sec has Carrer de Blai and the standing bars, and
        the vermut habit here is an ordinary weekday act rather than a revival.
        If you want one neighbourhood for one evening, take this one.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Gràcia</strong> eats like the independent town it was until
        1897 — small rooms opening onto a chain of squares, tables across the
        flagstones, and an atmosphere that is neighbourly rather than
        professional. <strong>Poblenou and the waterfront</strong> divide
        sharply: the beachfront strip is the weakest eating in Barcelona, while
        two streets inland Poblenou has become genuinely good, and Barceloneta's
        back lanes still hold proper seafood.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/bar-del-pi-sign.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/bar-del-pi-sign-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/bar-del-pi-sign-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/bar-del-pi-sign-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/bar-del-pi-sign-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/bar-del-pi-sign.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="The painted glass sign of Bar del Pi in Barcelona seen from below in low golden light, beneath an iron balcony with green shutters, with the striped and crossed flag of the city and the Spanish and European flags on a pole, and a yellow-rendered building with wooden shutters opposite."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="A square bar in the Barri Gòtic at the hour it is for. The test of a place like this is not the menu but whether the tables outside are filled by people who walked two streets to get there."
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="mistakes">Eight Common Mistakes</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Eight, because eight is what there is. Each of these costs a meal.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>1. Eating on La Rambla by default.</strong> The promenade's
        restaurants exist because a million people walk past who will not come
        back. Two streets either side is a different city and usually half the
        price.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>2. Calling everything tapas.</strong> It flattens five traditions
        and, more practically, it makes you order in the wrong units. Learn
        <em>platillo</em> and <em>ració</em> and the problem disappears.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>3. Hunting paella.</strong> Paella is Valencian, and Barcelona's
        own rice traditions — <em>arròs negre</em>, <em>fideuà</em> — are better
        bets here. A photograph of paella on a laminated board, a beachfront
        terrace, and a dish that arrives in twelve minutes are three signs of the
        same problem: it was not cooked for you. Rice to order takes half an hour
        and is normally for two.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>4. Building a day around one viral booking.</strong> A table
        secured six weeks ago in a distant neighbourhood will dictate an entire
        afternoon and is rarely worth the ground it costs. Barcelona's strength
        is depth, not peaks.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>5. Expecting dinner at seven.</strong> Covered above, and still
        the most common single error.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>6. Confusing a market with a restaurant hall.</strong> Municipal
        markets are shopping halls with some counters that cook. Arriving hungry
        at half past three expecting lunch will not work.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>7. Treating pintxos as Catalan.</strong> Enjoy them; just do not
        come away thinking you have eaten the regional cuisine, any more than a
        good curry in Glasgow explains Scotland.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>8. Crossing the city for a table.</strong> The metro ride you
        take at nine o'clock to reach a recommendation costs you the
        neighbourhood you were standing in, and the equivalent was usually two
        hundred metres away. This is the same argument the
        <a href="/best-things-to-do-in-barcelona/">experiences guide</a> makes
        about sightseeing, and it is truer of dinner.
      </p>

      <SectionHeading id="record">What the Documented Stays Actually Ate</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        Five establishments appear in the records of the 2023 and 2024 stays. What
        follows separates what is supported from what is not, because a food guide
        that invents its own meals is worth nothing. No exact order is written
        down for any of them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Quimet &amp; Quimet</strong>, Poble-sec — <em>visited and eaten
        at; format recorded, dishes not</em>. A standing bar with no seating,
        lined to the ceiling with bottles, where the food is
        <em>montaditos</em> built to order from conserves and preserves. Its own
        site records the bar as founded in <strong>1914</strong> and now in its
        <strong>fifth generation</strong>, taking no reservations and serving
        first-come, and — the operational fact that catches people out — opening
        <strong>Monday to Friday only</strong>, roughly midday to four and again
        from six. What the record supports: a sequence of montaditos and a glass
        poured from the wall of bottles. What it does not: which ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Xarcuteria La Pineda</strong>, Carrer del Pi 16 —
        <em>visited; photographed from the street; jamón and vermut recorded in
        general terms</em>. The photograph at the top of this page is the
        shopfront, which proves the door was reached and no more than that. The
        shop is documented in Catalonia's register of emblematic establishments
        as founded in <strong>1904</strong> and run by the Mir family since
        <strong>1930</strong>, retaining a 1920s glazed tile mosaic, a marble
        counter and a hand-cranked slicer. It is a xarcuteria with a few tables,
        not a restaurant.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Eldiset</strong>, Carrer de l'Argenteria, El Born —
        <em>visited and eaten at; dishes not recorded</em>. A wine bar with a
        Catalan list and small plates, and the recorded impression is of a long
        evening over shared plates rather than a meal built around any one dish.
        This is the sort of room the El Born paragraph above describes.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Suculent</strong>, Rambla del Raval —
        <em>visited and eaten at; dishes not recorded; and one correction</em>.
        The kitchen works traditional Catalan and Mediterranean dishes with
        modern technique and builds its reputation on slow-made broths and
        sauces. The correction is this: an earlier account on this site describes
        it as Michelin-starred. As checked on 4 August 2026, Suculent appears in
        the Michelin Guide but <strong>does not currently hold a star</strong>.
        It is a very good restaurant; it is not a starred one, and repeating the
        older claim would be the easiest kind of inherited error.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Caelum</strong>, Carrer de la Palla 8 —
        <em>recommended in the record; not evidenced as entered</em>. A café and
        shop selling sweets made in enclosed convents, above a medieval bath
        chamber that is visible from the lower room. It appears in the week's
        notes as a recommendation rather than an account of a visit, and is
        listed as trading by both its own site and the city council's register.
        Treated here as researched, not eaten.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Practical place="Barcelona — At the Table" detail="Verified 4 August 2026" detailIcon="🍽">
      <PracticalSection label="The hours that actually matter" level={3} id="hours">
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Lunch</strong> from about 14:00, busy to 15:30.
          <strong>Dinner</strong> from about 21:30. A 19:00 dinner narrows you to
          places cooking for visitors.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Menú del dia is weekday lunch only.</strong> It does not exist
          in the evening and rarely at weekends.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Market counters close early.</strong> Produce stalls are best
          before eleven; by mid-afternoon much of a market is shuttered.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Sundays are thin</strong> — La Boqueria shuts entirely, and
          many neighbourhood kitchens close Sunday evening and Monday.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Market days to plan around" level={3}>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>La Boqueria</strong> — Monday to Saturday 08:00–20:30, closed
          Sundays and public holidays.
          <a href="https://www.boqueria.barcelona/" rel="noopener">Official site</a>.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Mercat de Sant Antoni</strong> — <strong>closed
          Tuesdays</strong>; stalls broadly 10:00–20:00.
          <a href="https://www.mercatdesantantoni.com/en/" rel="noopener">Official site</a>.
        </div>
        <div class="prac-item">
          <strong>Santa Caterina and La Llibertat</strong> — both keep
          early-closing days and are safest treated as morning markets. The
          municipal markets body publishes current hours for all thirty-nine on
          <a href="https://ajuntament.barcelona.cat/mercats/en" rel="noopener">Mercats
          de Barcelona</a>.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Ordering, in practice" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Two people:</strong> four or five platillos, or two racions and
          bread. Order in rounds rather than all at once.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Keep your pintxo sticks.</strong> They are how the bill is
          counted.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Terrace surcharges are normal</strong> and should be posted.
          Standing at the counter is often the cheapest and always the most
          local option.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Rice takes half an hour</strong> and is usually for two. If it
          arrives fast, it was not made for you.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Catalan on the menu is a good sign</strong> — not proof of
          quality, but a reasonable indicator of who the room expects.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>

      <PracticalSection label="Seasonal notes" level={3}>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Calçots:</strong> roughly November to April, peaking January to
          March, and properly a countryside meal inland from Tarragona.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>August:</strong> the city eats later, and a number of
          family-run kitchens close for two or three weeks. Check before
          travelling across town.
        </div>
        <div class="tip-row">
          <strong>Salt cod salads</strong> like esqueixada belong to the hot
          months; the heavy stews and cap i pota belong to the cold ones.
        </div>
      </PracticalSection>
    </Practical>
  </div>

  <div class="post-closing">
    <div class="narrative prose" style="max-width:820px; margin:0 auto;">
      <p>
        Almost everything that goes wrong at a table in Barcelona comes from one
        of two errors, and neither is about choosing badly. The first is the
        clock: eating at hours the city does not eat at, which quietly restricts
        you to rooms that serve visitors. The second is the category mistake:
        walking into a xarcuteria expecting dinner, a market expecting a
        restaurant, or a pintxos bar expecting Catalonia.
      </p>
      <p>
        Fix those two and the city becomes remarkably forgiving. There are
        thirty-nine food markets, a dense layer of small kitchens in half a dozen
        neighbourhoods, and a fixed-price lunch in every district that will feed
        you better than most things you could have booked. Learn six words, arrive
        at two and at half past nine, order the vegetables as seriously as the
        meat, and stay in the neighbourhood you are already standing in. The
        rest is detail — and the longer version of this city is
        <a href="/7-days-in-barcelona/">a week at its table</a>, at the end of
        <a href="/4-weeks-portugal-to-spain-by-rail-road/">a month overland from
        Porto</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

